A||||3||94ZonCrack||Cleaning engine internals||||5||
Z||000000||94ZonCrack||06-18-2001||05:06 PM||||Ok I've bought a new gasktet set. This is actually for an 87 4.1L caddy. I'm doing the caddy swap. I was wondering whats the best method to clean internal engine parts, like the valvetrain, intake? I was thinking of just spraying using a bunch of carb cleaner. Is theere a better way? Appreciate any input.||||reg||5
Z||000001||Leadfoot||06-18-2001||06:56 PM||||I went to NAPA and bought a 5 gal barrel ($25) of parts cleaner. I then went to Walmart and bought a big plastic container ($10) and put 1/4 of the cleaner in it at a time.  You might want to go to Ace hardware and pick up some steel and brass wire brushes.  To clean the intake I rigged a brush used to clean the refrigerator coil on my power drill.  Give your parts a final rinse with fresh cleaner and blow them dry with compressed air.   <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rockcrawl||06-18-2001||09:29 PM||||Pressure washer!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> Seriously, that's what I use on mine. Get in there and blast everything out, then blow out all the oil passages with compressed air and let it sit in the sun for a while. It helps to wear a Tyvec paint suit or else you'll end up ruining your clothing! The small parts, pistons, rockers, etc. I clean in a parts washer. If you're not going to disassemble the entire engine, it may not be a good idea though. The motor should look something like this when you're done.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/45PFI1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by rockcrawl (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000003||Phil||06-18-2001||10:00 PM||||Almost all of those parts cleaning solutions are just mineral spirits. Cheaper to buy the stuff as mineral spirits.||||reg||1
